Postpartum hemorrhage
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Massive postpartum vaginal bleeding, Boggy non-contracted uterus, Hemodynamic instability

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-038
Reason for Visit: Acute, massive vaginal bleeding with passage of large clots causing severe presyncope 5 days post-vaginal delivery.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden onset of catastrophic vaginal hemorrhage (soaking a maternity pad every 15 minutes) and impending hypovolemic shock (dizziness, pallor) in a 5-day postpartum patient. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately initiate bimanual uterine compression, administer pharmacological uterotonics, and coordinate emergency priority 1 ambulance transport to save the patient's life before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had my baby 5 days ago, and suddenly blood is gushing out of me, I am passing clots the size of oranges, and I can't stay awake.
History of Present Illness: The patient, 5 days post-uncomplicated vaginal delivery, reports a sudden, massive increase in vaginal bleeding exceeding normal lochia. She describes a continuous large gush of bright red blood from the vagina, soaking through heavy maternity pads in under 15 minutes. She reports the passage of multiple large, organized blood clots. She complains of severe dizziness upon attempting to stand, profound generalized weakness, and blurred vision, indicating acute hemodynamic instability. She denies fever or foul-smelling discharge.
Functional Status: Supine on the floor; unable to safely sit or stand due to severe presyncope and weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent life-threatening hemorrhagic shock.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 mmHg | HR: 135 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly pale, diaphoretic, lethargic, and exhibiting altered mental status (confusion/drowsiness).
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, boggy, and poorly contracted uterus palpated transabdominally (uterine atony). Visual inspection of the perineum confirms active, continuous bright red bleeding with large clots present. The perineum is intact without obvious external lacerations. Signs of impending hypovolemic shock are evident: severe maternal tachycardia, progressive hypotension, pallor, and cold clammy skin.
Environmental Observations: Floor and clothing heavily saturated with fresh blood.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Secondary Postpartum Hemorrhage (Severe Uterine Atony vs. Retained Placental Fragments) - Status: Hemorrhagic Shock
Differentials:

Uterine Atony - The primary physical finding (boggy uterus), causing massive bleeding due to failure of myometrial vessel constriction.

Retained Placental Tissue - The most common underlying cause of secondary PPH; necrotic tissue sloughs, exposing vessels and preventing sustained atony. Requires hospital ultrasound and D&C.

Coagulopathy - A secondary complication; the massive bleeding consumes clotting factors, worsening the hemorrhage in a vicious cycle.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Critical threat to life)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of catastrophic hemorrhagic shock due to a massive secondary postpartum hemorrhage. The objective clinical evidence of a boggy, non-contracted uterus combined with active, massive vaginal bleeding and profound hemodynamic instability confirms a life-threatening emergency. Urgent after-hours attendance was critical to provide immediate, aggressive mechanical intervention (fundal massage) to physically compress the bleeding vessels, administer available hemostatic agents, and orchestrate an immediate "load and go" ambulance transfer for massive transfusion and surgical management. This intervention was life-saving and could not be delayed.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for a "Priority 1 - Hemorrhagic Shock / PPH" response. Positioned the patient supine with legs elevated. Initiated immediate, aggressive, and continuous transabdominal fundal massage to expel clots and mechanically stimulate uterine contraction. Administered intramuscular Oxytocin 10 units and oral Tranexamic Acid 1g. Placed a large-bore (14G) IV cannula to expedite fluid resuscitation by paramedics upon arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain vital blood flow to your brain, remain completely flat on your back and do not lift your head or sit up; recognizing that this prevents you from losing consciousness will ensure strict compliance. To assist the emergency response, allow the paramedics to physically move you onto the stretcher without your help; understanding that physical exertion worsens bleeding will keep you still.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on scene.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding intensive care paramedics.

